'''Ominira''' is the capital and largest city of the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]], located on the western half of the island of [[Bolodun]]. It occupies roughly 147 square kilometres, about 48 percent of Bolodun's land area, and had an estimated population of 56,394 in 2025. Ominira is the seat of the [[President of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|presidency]] and the [[People's Assembly of Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|People's Assembly]].

The city is built to a distinctive raised design following the reconstruction after [[Hurricane Sahan]]: buildings are elevated on stilts or columns, with public life conducted on the second floor and the open ground level used for moving goods in dry weather and sheltering from storm surge during heavy rains.

== History ==

Ominira grew as the principal settlement of Bolodun, the centre of the islands' sugar plantation economy. Its port is artificial, since Bolodun has no natural harbours, and was built to serve the sugar export trade.

In 1969, [[Hurricane Sahan]] destroyed virtually the entire city, levelling Ominira and the island's infrastructure. The subsequent [[Adonian Community]]-led reconstruction rebuilt the city to a raised, hurricane-resistant design, with heavy investment from [[Burawa]] in the 1970s. Since then, most urban construction in Ominira has been elevated above flood and storm-surge level.

In 2019, [[Hurricane Nadira]] struck the islands with moderate damage in Ominira. The city's raised construction limited the impact compared with the 1969 disaster.

== Government ==

Ominira is the seat of the national government. The [[President of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|president]] is based at the Presidential Palace in the city, and the [[People's Assembly of Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|People's Assembly]] meets in Ominira.

== Economy ==

Ominira is the economic centre of the country. The city's artificial port handles the islands' trade, and the state provides free public healthcare, education, and transportation to all citizens. The city has capitalized on its education system to become a destination for medical tourism, drawing patients from across the Capuyaquiran Sea. The country's main international airport, [[Ominira International Airport]], lies on the eastern half of Bolodun, connected to the city by road.

